Comments by Bruce Char, general chair of ISSAC'97:

HOTEL ROOMS

The Aston Wailea has over 500 rooms, divided between a seven story
high rise and about six or seven low-rise (three story) buildings.  I
was put into an ocean-front room, about 25 yards from the shore.
The view from the room was quite nice, an unobstructed view of the
ocean, the west Maui mountains, and the islands of Lanai, Kahoolawe, and
Molokini, which are about five-ten miles away.

This was of course a particularly nice room.  The "ordinary price"
rooms have a view of the mountain (Haleakala) instead, but are
probably no further than 200 yards from the shore.  I saw a standard
room and it had the usual features: air conditioning/sliding door to
the outdoor air, two double beds or one king, TV, dresser, closet,
lamps, bath, iron, phone, clothesline.  Each room has a small
refrigerator without minibar an important feature.  Ice and vending
machines in each building.

Being used to hotels in cities, the quietness of the location and of
the rooms was welcome.  Of course, if you open the window, you get to
hear the ocean.


HOTEL GROUNDS

Overall, quiet pleasant.  The grounds are spacious and
well-maintained: grass, coconut trees, bird of paradise and other
flowering plants, etc.  At night there are spotlights illuminating
parts of the ocean, although when I was there the moon did a
reasonable job of illumination all by itself.  Other than the hotel
restaurant and lounge (rock music/dancing nightly), there are a number
of good places for quiet conversation: the open air hotel lobby, the
beach chairs along the shore, and the "luau pool".  The main swimming
pool (overlooks the ocean itself) has an adjacent bar and casual
restaurant.  There is Hawaiian music at the main pool at sunset.  The
hotel, 25 years old, is in reasonable shape with recent and on-going
renovations of its grounds and rooms.  It has a casual, open,
architecture.

The hotel's shoreline is rocky with coral reefs.  People go out during
low tide to walk on the reef and look at the marine life, or go scuba
diving or snorkeling during high tide.  The hotel grounds are
immediately adjacent to two beaches, which are public as are all other
beaches under Hawaiian state law.  Both have excellent swimming. It
was great to walk two minutes along the shoreline path to the beach
for a quick dip.

The shoreline path connects the hotel to the beaches, as well as to the
other resorts and condos in the Wailea development.  Following the
path, one can walk in a few minutes to additional beaches, the Grand
Wailea Resort, the Renaissance Hotel, the Four Seasons Hotel, and the
Kea Lani Hotel.

GROUND TRANSPORTATION

Within walking distance:
