GETTING TO/FROM the airport

	They recommend car rental, although there is a shuttle bus
	("Speedy Shuttle" I think it's called, but this needs to be
	checked out) which costs $17/person OW.

FOOD, RESTAURANTS

The hotel food was decent but on the pricey side.  I had lunch at the
poolside restaurant, and breakfast in the main dining room. Breakfast
would run $10-$20.  Lunch about the same.  Dinner would be $20 and
up.

Within walking distance are:

The fifteen restaurants of the nearby hotels (Renaissance Wailea
Beach, Grand Wailea, Four Seasons, Kea Lani),

Four more dinner/lunch restaurants
A sub shop

In addition to those restaurants, there are five more, plus the various
bars at the hotels and golf and tennis clubs.  These you need
to drive to.  I ate dinner at the Seawatch, which is upslope enough from
the shore to have a dramatic view from its balcony to the ocean and the
islands offshore.

In short, people with cash will find no shortage of interesting
restaurants of great variety.  (There is a cash machine in the hotel
lobby for people with a cash shortage.) For people with a modest
budget, the sub shop and the Sandcastle are probably the most
reasonably priced places nearby.  The ten minute drive into Kihei will
allow one to dine much more cheaply if one wishes: Pizza Hut,
McDonalds, IHOP, Thai, Chinese, Korean, Japanese, Pacific Rim,
Italian, Ribs, various sandwich cafes, and a few pricier restaurants
with good reputations.  There is a coffee house with an internet
connection (I read my email there) where one can get a croissant and a
cafe au lait for $5.

There are at least two large supermarkets in Kihei.

TOURISM

	Each morning at around 8:15 the hotel has a briefing of the
day's activities.  The hotel would be happy to arrange a group
briefing for attendees and/or their families at the start of the
conferences.  There is of course no shortage of tours you can go on.
Beach equipment (snorkel, sailboard, scuba) is also easy to rent.  It
is possible to rent a car at two of the nearby hotels: Budget at the
Grand Wailea, and Avis at the Renaissance.  There are also sessions on
Hawaiian arts and crafts.

OFF-SITE ACCOMMODATIONS

There's no apparent shortage of hotel/condo accommodations in Kihei.
These would probably be cheaper than staying at the hotel.  I believe
local arrangements already has the phone numbers of several condo brokers
and I believe the tourism magazines I brought back have the names of
some more.
